    "fact_rationales": {
      "F11": "Low-frequency tremor is more typical of cerebellar disorders, but can also be seen in multiple sclerosis.",
      "F13": "Overshooting the target is a classic sign of cerebellar dysfunction, making cerebellar demyelination more likely.",
      "F14": "Increased tone in the lower extremities can be seen in both multiple sclerosis and cerebellar demyelination, but is more typical of the former.",
      "F15": "Positive Babinski's sign is more commonly associated with multiple sclerosis, which can cause upper motor neuron signs."
    },
